@page "/docs/get-started/from-radzen"

@* Moving an app from Radzen.Blazor: running both side by side, setup, a component mapping, then
   buttons, data grids, fields, dialogs, toasts and drag and drop with the renamed members and what
   behaves differently. Radzen names checked against Radzen's API docs; IzzyBlazor's against its
   source and components.json. *@

<ArticlePage Section="get-started" Slug="from-radzen">
    <Lead>
        Most Radzen components have an IzzyBlazor counterpart with a similar API, so a page usually moves over by renaming
        components and members. The larger change is where state lives: sorting, filters and paging go in the URL, and
        forms post on static pages.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Running both libraries</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The two libraries can run side by side while you move page by page, as in
            <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/existing-app">Add to an existing app</Anchor>. Some types have the same name in
            both: <Code>DialogService</Code>, <Code>DialogOptions</Code>, <Code>ConfirmOptions</Code>, <Code>Dialog</Code>,
            <Code>Tooltip</Code>, <Code>TextAlign</Code>, <Code>Orientation</Code>, <Code>IconStyle</Code>,
            <Code>SidebarPosition</Code> and <Code>ColorScheme</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A file with <Code>@@using</Code> for both libraries gets ambiguous references. Move a file to IzzyBlazor whole, or
            qualify the name (<Code>IzzyBlazor.DialogService</Code>). Components of your own can clash too, such as a
            <Code>Menu</Code>. Rename yours, or replace it with IzzyBlazor's.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Setup</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Replace Radzen's services, stylesheet, script and <Code>&lt;RadzenComponents /&gt;</Code>. IzzyBlazor's theme is
            <Code>&lt;IzzyTheme /&gt;</Code> in the head. There's no script tag, because Blazor loads
            <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">IzzyBlazor's script</Anchor> itself.
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(SetupRadzen, SetupIzzy)
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>DialogHost</Code> is where <Code>DialogService</Code> shows its dialogs, and it must be interactive. When
            <Code>Routes</Code> is interactive, put it in the layout as it is. Otherwise, give it a render mode:
            <Code>&lt;DialogHost @@rendermode="InteractiveServer" /&gt;</Code>. <Code>ToastRegion</Code> is where
            <Code>ToastService</Code> shows its toasts, and it works in any render mode.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Replace <Code>@@using Radzen</Code> and <Code>@@using Radzen.Blazor</Code> in <Code>_Imports.razor</Code> with
            <Code>@@using IzzyBlazor</Code>, and remove any stylesheets you wrote to adjust Radzen's <Code>rz-</Code> classes.
            For the rest of setup, such as your brand color in <Code>IzzyTheme</Code>, see
            <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/blazor-web-app">Blazor Web App</Anchor> and
            <Anchor Href="docs/theming/themes">Themes and presets</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Components</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Each Radzen component below maps to the IzzyBlazor component that does the same job. IzzyBlazor's form fields
            render their own label, hint and errors, so a <Code>RadzenFormField</Code> or <Code>RadzenLabel</Code> around
            them goes away.
        </TextBlock>
        <Table Items="Components" Caption="Radzen components and their IzzyBlazor counterparts" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Radzen" Context="row">@Rich(row.Radzen)</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="IzzyBlazor" Context="row">@Rich(row.Izzy)</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Notes" Context="row">@Rich(row.Note)</TemplateColumn>
    
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            See <Anchor Href="docs/components">Components</Anchor> for every component, including ones Radzen doesn't have,
            such as <Code>Diff</Code> and <Code>FilterForm</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">State in the URL</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A Radzen grid keeps its sort, page and filters in the component. A <Code>Table</Code> or <Code>DataGrid</Code>
            keeps them in the URL. A sortable header is a link that sets <Code>?sort=email</Code>, page numbers set
            <Code>?page=2</Code>, and a filter sets <Code>?f-email=</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            The view survives a reload and can be shared, and the back button undoes a sort. It also works on static pages
            and with JavaScript off. Two tables on one page share these keys unless one has a <Code>QueryPrefix</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            To keep the state in the component instead, as Radzen does, bind it on interactive pages:
            <Code>@@bind-Sort</Code>, <Code>@@bind-Page</Code> and <Code>@@bind-Filter</Code>. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">Render modes</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Forms on static pages</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            IzzyBlazor's components also work on static pages, so not every page needs an interactive render mode. An app
            that renders every page interactively keeps working as before.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>Form</Code> takes the place of <Code>RadzenTemplateForm</Code>. It wraps Blazor's <Code>EditForm</Code>:
            on interactive pages it validates as the user edits, and on static pages it sends a form post, even with
            JavaScript off. A form that posts on static pages needs a <Code>FormName</Code>; see
            <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms">Forms and validation</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Buttons</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>RadzenButton</Code> becomes <Code>Button</Code>, with its text as content. A button with only an icon is an
            <Code>IconButton</Code>, which requires a <Code>Label</Code> for screen readers. Icons are Lucide's
            (<Code>Lucide.X</Code>), not Material names.
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(ButtonsRadzen, ButtonsIzzy)
        <Table Items="ButtonRenames" Caption="RadzenButton members" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="RadzenButton" Context="row">@Rich(row.Radzen)</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Button" Context="row">@Rich(row.Izzy)</TemplateColumn>
    
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>id</Code>, <Code>aria-label</Code> and other attributes go on the <Code>button</Code> element, as before, so
            selectors such as <Code>#key-create</Code> keep working. <Code>Loading</Code> shows a spinner and keeps the button
            focusable. <Code>Disabled</Code> alone gives no sign that something is happening. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/button">Button</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Data grids</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>RadzenDataGrid</Code> becomes a <Code>DataGrid</Code>, or a <Code>Table</Code> for a plain list of records.
            Both accept the same columns. A column bound to a property is a <Code>PropertyColumn</Code>, which accepts an
            expression instead of the property's name. A column with a template is a <Code>TemplateColumn</Code>, whose
            content is the template. There's no <Code>Columns</Code> wrapper, and <Code>TItem</Code> comes from
            <Code>Items</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(GridRadzen, GridIzzy)
        <Table Items="GridRenames" Caption="RadzenDataGrid members" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="RadzenDataGrid" Context="row">@Rich(row.Radzen)</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="DataGrid and Table" Context="row">@Rich(row.Izzy)</TemplateColumn>
    
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>What behaves differently:</TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Columns opt in.</strong> Radzen sorts and filters every column unless told not to. A column here sorts
                only with <Code>Sortable</Code> and filters only with <Code>Filterable</Code>.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>A template column sorts by what you set.</strong> Radzen sorts a template column by its
                <Code>Property</Code>. A <Code>TemplateColumn</Code> requires <Code>SortBy</Code> and a <Code>SortKey</Code>
                for the URL to sort, and <Code>FilterBy</Code> to filter.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Filters open from the header.</strong> A filterable column has a filter button in its header, marked
                while the column is filtered. To add a row of filter fields under the headers, set <Code>FilterRow</Code> on
                a <Code>DataGrid</Code>. To let the user choose the operator, set <Code>FilterOperators</Code> on a column; a
                <Code>DataGrid</Code>'s columns offer the choice by default. Text filters ignore case unless you set
                <Code>FilterCase</Code>.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Selection is a checkbox column, kept by key.</strong> Each row has a checkbox, or a radio button for
                <Code>Single</Code>, rather than selecting on a row click. The table keeps the rows' <Code>ItemKey</Code>, not
                the items, so a selection survives paging and reloading. Shift-click selects a range.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Selected rows get a bar of actions.</strong> The footer counts what's selected, and
                <Code>SelectionActions</Code> appear in a bar above the rows. <Code>SelectAllMode</Code> sets whether the header
                checkbox selects the page or every row the filters match. In a form, the selection posts, so a bulk action can
                be a submit button.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong><Code>Caption</Code> names the table</strong> for screen readers, and isn't shown. An <Code>id</Code>
                goes on the table's outer element, so <Code>#members tr</Code> still finds the rows.
            </ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>LoadData</Code> becomes an <Code>ItemsProvider</Code>, which loads each page from a server. Its
            <Code>DataRequest</Code> carries the sort, the range and the filter, and <Code>ToResultAsync</Code> applies them
            to an EF Core query, as <Code>LoadDataArgs</Code> with Dynamic LINQ did. The provider can also page by an opaque
            "next" cursor, with no total. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/table/loading-data#data-from-a-database">Data from a database</Anchor> and
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/table/loading-data#paging-without-a-total">Paging without a total</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Fields</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>RadzenTextBox</Code>, <Code>RadzenNumeric</Code>, <Code>RadzenDropDown</Code> and the other inputs become
            fields: <Code>TextField</Code>, <Code>NumberField</Code>, <Code>Select</Code> and so on. Each field renders its
            label, hint and errors, and links them for screen readers. An <Code>id</Code> goes on the input.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">@FieldsIzzy</Code>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>The value changes when the field does</strong>: when the user leaves it or presses Enter, as with
                Blazor's <Code>InputText</Code>. To react as the user types, add an <Code>@@oninput</Code> handler to the
                field. It goes to the input.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Errors come from the form.</strong> A field shows its <Code>EditContext</Code>'s messages for its
                property. In a <Code>Form</Code>, add an API's error to a <Code>ValidationMessageStore</Code>, and the field
                shows it. Outside a form, show the message yourself.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>A <Code>DateField</Code> reads and shows times in UTC</strong> unless you set <Code>TimeZone</Code>.
                Bind a <Code>DateTimeOffset</Code> for a date and time, or a <Code>DateOnly</Code> for a date alone. A
                <Code>DateTime</Code> works too. One that isn't <Code>Local</Code> is read as UTC, and on interactive pages it
                comes back with <Code>DateTimeKind.Utc</Code>.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>A <Code>Select</Code>'s items are its values.</strong> Pass them as <Code>Items</Code> and set their
                text with <Code>ItemText</Code>. There's no <Code>ValueProperty</Code>, so to bind an id, pass the ids as
                <Code>Items</Code>.
            </ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            See <Anchor Href="docs/components/text-field">TextField</Anchor>,
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/select">Select</Anchor>,
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/date-field">DateField</Anchor> and
            <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms">Forms and validation</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Dialogs</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>DialogService</Code> keeps its name and its <Code>OpenAsync&lt;T&gt;(title, parameters, options)</Code>.
            It returns a <Code>DialogResult</Code> instead of <Code>dynamic</Code>. <Code>Data</Code> is what the dialog closed
            with, and <Code>Canceled</Code> is true when the user dismissed it.
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(OpenRadzen, OpenIzzy, language: "csharp")
        <TextBlock>
            The width is a <Code>Length</Code>, or one of the named sizes: <Code>Sm</Code> is 384px, <Code>Md</Code> (the
            default) 512px, <Code>Lg</Code> 768px and <Code>Xl</Code> 1024px. On a phone, the dialog still fits the screen.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Inside the dialog, close it through the <Code>DialogInstance</Code> it cascades, not through the service.
            <Code>Close(data)</Code> ends <Code>OpenAsync</Code> with that data, and <Code>Cancel()</Code> ends it as
            canceled. A Cancel button that reports something, such as how many people were added before one was refused,
            calls <Code>Close</Code> with that instead.
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(InsideRadzen, InsideIzzy)
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>DialogService.Close(data)</Code> still exists and closes the most recent dialog, as Radzen's does.
            <Code>DialogInstance</Code> closes only its own dialog. Closing a dialog that has already ended does nothing, so a
            request that comes back after its dialog closed can't close the next one.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>Confirm</Code> becomes <Code>ConfirmAsync</Code>, with the same message, title and options. It returns a
            <Code>bool</Code> rather than a <Code>bool?</Code>. To make the confirm button red for a removal, set
            <Code>Accent.Danger</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(ConfirmRadzen, ConfirmIzzy, language: "csharp")
        <Table Items="DialogRenames" Caption="DialogService members" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Radzen" Context="row">@Rich(row.Radzen)</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="IzzyBlazor" Context="row">@Rich(row.Izzy)</TemplateColumn>
    
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>What behaves differently:</TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Focus moves in as the dialog opens.</strong> The dialog is the browser's own (<Code>showModal</Code>),
                so focus goes to the field with <Code>autofocus</Code>, or else the first control that can take focus.
                Radzen moves focus half a second later.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Every dismissal is a cancel.</strong> Escape, a click on the backdrop and the close button all end
                <Code>OpenAsync</Code> with <Code>Canceled</Code> true and no data.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>A confirm starts on Cancel.</strong> Cancel has the focus, and Escape cancels. A confirm has no close
                button and ignores clicks on the backdrop, so the user answers with one of its buttons or Escape.
            </ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            See <Anchor Href="docs/components/dialog">Dialog</Anchor>, which also covers dialogs written in the page.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Notifications</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>NotificationService</Code> becomes <Code>ToastService</Code>, and its toasts appear in the layout's
            <Code>ToastRegion</Code>. Call <Code>Success</Code>, <Code>Info</Code>, <Code>Warning</Code> or
            <Code>Error</Code> with the message, and an optional title:
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(NotifyRadzen, NotifyIzzy, language: "csharp")
        <TextBlock>
            Toasts work in every render mode. A toast sent while a static page renders appears in that page, and one sent
            before a redirect appears on the next page. Error toasts stay until the user closes them. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/toast-region">ToastRegion</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Drag and drop</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A <Code>RadzenDropZoneContainer</Code> with <Code>RadzenDropZone</Code>s becomes a <Code>DropZoneGroup</Code>
            with a <Code>ListView</Code> per zone. As in Radzen, the group has one list of items.
        </TextBlock>
        <Table Items="DropRenames" Caption="RadzenDropZoneContainer members" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Radzen" Context="row">@Rich(row.Radzen)</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="IzzyBlazor" Context="row">@Rich(row.Izzy)</TemplateColumn>
    
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>OnMove</Code> gets <Code>FromZone</Code>, <Code>ToZone</Code> and the item's place in each. The zone shows
            the item in its new place before you've saved it.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Each item's handle opens a menu with the same moves: Move to a zone, and up and down. So moving works with the
            keyboard, on touch, and with JavaScript off. To reorder a single list, use a <Code>ListView</Code> with
            <Code>Reorderable</Code>. See <Anchor Href="docs/components/drop-zone-group">DropZoneGroup</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components">Components</Anchor>: Stepper, SegmentedControl and the rest of what you may have built by hand.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">Render modes</Anchor>: what changes if some pages become static.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/theming/tokens">Tokens and customizing</Anchor>: CSS variables for your own styles beside the components.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    // Radzen's code on the left, IzzyBlazor's on the right; one under the other until the content is
    // wide enough for both.
    private static RenderFragment SideBySide(string before, string after, string beforeTitle = "Radzen", string language = "razor") =>
        @<Grid MinChildWidth="Length.Rem(26)" Gap="3">
            <Code Block Language="@language" Title="@beforeTitle">@before</Code>
            <Code Block Language="@language" Title="IzzyBlazor">@after</Code>
        </Grid>;

    private sealed record Mapping(string Radzen, string Izzy, string Note = "");

    private static Mapping Map(string radzen, string izzy, string note = "") => new(radzen, izzy, note);

    // A table cell: text, with the parts between backticks as code.
    private static RenderFragment Rich(string text) => builder =>
    {
        var parts = text.Split('`');
        for (var i = 0; i < parts.Length; i++)
        {
            if (i % 2 == 1)
            {
                builder.OpenElement(0, "code");
                builder.AddContent(1, parts[i]);
                builder.CloseElement();
            }
            else
            {
                builder.AddContent(2, parts[i]);
            }
        }
    };

